WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.279 --> 00:00:05.290
You were once a kicking, screaming, tiny little
human.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.849
I know, it’s hard to believe, right?

00:00:07.849 --> 00:00:12.309
Since you probably can’t remember a single
thing about being a baby.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:16.390
Most of your early childhood is probably a
memory-less void, and the few events you do

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.119
remember are likely hazy.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:24.580
This phenomenon is known as “childhood amnesia”
and it happens to everyone.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.970
Some people are better at remembering than
others but we’re all pretty bad at it.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:33.260
But why is it so hard to remember life as
a baby?

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.149
Let’s find out.

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:43.199
It’s believed that memories start to form
in our brain as soon as we’re born and maybe

00:00:43.199 --> 00:00:48.910
even before, however, studies have found that
children don’t actually store those memories

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.429
permanently, so that they can later be accessed
in adult life.

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:59.079
We don’t start storing memories in a somewhat
semi-permanent manner until the ages of four

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:00.210
to seven.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.320
We can usually remember certain important
events that occurred between the ages of four

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.340
and seven.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.180
But before the age of four, nothing.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.009
In fact, during a study, children between
the ages of four and seven were told things

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:19.390
that they definitely did and said two years
prior, but none of the children could remember

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.690
doing or saying them.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:26.590
Most of the children actually said “that
wasn’t me, it was someone else”.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:32.140
Infamous neurologist and pioneering phycologist
Sigmund Freud believed that we repress our

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:38.009
earliest childhood memories because this period
of time is so full of psychosexual content

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:43.950
that we simply wouldn’t be able to handle
such disturbing memories, even as an adult.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.479
But nowadays that theory carries very little
gravitas.

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:52.570
More recently however new theories have emerged
that attempt to once and for all explain this

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.159
mysterious childhood memory loss.

00:01:55.159 --> 00:02:00.670
The first of these is that as an infant our
brain quite simply isn’t sufficiently developed

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:01.670
yet.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.170
The two parts of our brain necessary for forming
new memories, the hippocampus and the medial

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:10.369
temporal lobe are already well developed by
the time we’re one-year-old.

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:16.540
However, another part of our brain called
the prefrontal cortex doesn’t fully develop

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.980
until our early twenties.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:24.459
It is believed the prefrontal cortex is responsible
for creating episodic memories.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:27.200
Which are memories of actual events.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.879
Being able to remember what happened, where
it happened and roughly at what time it happened

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:37.160
is a skill our brains don’t master until
later on in life.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.510
Until this time our brain is only able to
remember skills we’ve learnt as well as

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.799
cosmetic things such as items and faces.

00:02:44.799 --> 00:02:50.409
As babies we aren’t able to attach emotions
to these things, so they don’t transform

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:54.760
into events and are therefore less memorable.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.970
Another theory that compounds this effect
is that as babies we aren’t able to talk.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.900
We have no language.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:07.210
That’s quite significant, because it means
we were never able to represent memories with

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.340
words.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.700
For example, if you think back to the day
when you moved into a new house your can probably

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:18.860
remember distinguishable aspects of that day,
such as, the house had six windows and a blue

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.000
door and you arrived at your new house in
a small red car.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.090
If an infant were to be placed in the exact
same situation it wouldn’t be able to attach

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:33.150
words to the event because a baby has no idea
what a window or a door is, never mind how

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.760
to describe them.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:40.269
So the infant wouldn’t be able to make that
all-important link between words and memories,

00:03:40.269 --> 00:03:43.470
which is what we do all the time as adults.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:48.041
Psychologists think that by attaching language
to memories in this way, it helps our brain

00:03:48.041 --> 00:03:50.390
to organise and store them more efficiently.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:56.550
So, if we’re able to describe a thing or
an event with words it’s considerably more

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.519
likely that our brain will remember it several
years down the line.

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:06.049
By the time the average child reaches four
years old it knows about 5,000 words.

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:11.219
That’s just enough to be able to describe
most things and events.

00:04:11.219 --> 00:04:15.219
Even if those descriptions are rather basic,
it doesn’t matter, it’s still fuel being

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:17.100
added to the memory tank.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.180
This process of attaching words to memories
is further reinforced by the fact that when

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:27.540
we learn a language, we’re then able to
talk about things we’ve seen, with others.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.270
And every time we talk about something that’s
happened, we’re much more likely to remember

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.470
it in the future.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:37.419
That’s why your granddad has told you that
exact same story about when he was a young

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:42.199
lad a hundred bloody times and continues to
do so every time you see him.

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:45.570
Because he’s told it so many times, that
it’s nigh on impossible for his brain to

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.960
forget it.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.509
These theories do have some weight to them
and they’re widely regarded as some as the

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:55.009
best theories we currently have to explain
why we can’t remember life as a toddler.

00:04:55.009 --> 00:05:00.460
But there’s brand new evidence that may
once and for all, definitively explain this

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:01.460
phenomenon.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:06.470
We all know just how much scientists love
to answer questions by experimenting on rats

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:09.389
and this question is no exception.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:12.940
In an experiment scientists controlled the
speed at which rat’s brains were able to

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.650
make new brain cells.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:19.300
They found that when they slowed down the
rat’s ability to generate new brain cells

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.699
the rats were able to form more solid, clearer
memories and they were able to recall memories

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:25.789
easier.

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:31.230
Conversely, when they sped-up the rat’s
ability to make new brain cells the rats were

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.699
more forgetful and their memories became fuzzier.

00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:37.710
Now before you ask, how they were able to
test the rat’s memories?

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:41.110
Let me explain, I mean they couldn’t exactly
interview them, could they?

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:46.669
The scientists did this by associating a place
with a mild electric shock, then observing

00:05:46.669 --> 00:05:50.860
how the rats reacted when they returned to
that same place in the future.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.169
But shouldn’t it be the other way around?

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:58.569
Shouldn’t the rats who had their brain cell
production rate increased, have been able

00:05:58.569 --> 00:06:01.780
to remember more, not less?

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.020
You would think so, but here’s why it’s
actually the opposite way around.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.090
Just like a computer hard drive, our brains
have limited storage space.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:16.520
There’s only room for so many brain cells,
so our ability to store memories is limited.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.789
This means that when new brain cells are created,
if there’s no more space remaining, then

00:06:20.789 --> 00:06:23.270
they have to overwrite old ones.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:27.889
So when a new memory is created, if there
isn’t any spare room our brain decides which

00:06:27.889 --> 00:06:33.860
memories are useless and can therefore be
forgotten and deletes them to make room for

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:34.860
the new memory.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:40.090
Oh and before you say we’re born with so
many brain cells and we can’t grow new ones,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.889
that rumour simply isn’t true.

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:46.550
Research has shown that brain cells and neurons
continue to grow in our brains throughout

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.940
our entire lives, although the process does
slow down somewhat as we age.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:57.270
So why does this deletion of memories occur
more often as an infant than as an adult?

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:02.360
Two reasons, the first is that our brains
are developing at an astonishingly fast rate

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.360
as a child.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.750
We are learning and developing faster than
we ever will in the remainder of our life.

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.090
So a lot of room needs to be made for new
memories and skills we learn.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:18.069
Secondly, a lot of what we experience as an
infant is deemed non-essential by our brain.

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:22.020
Because let’s face it, you don’t need
to remember the time you were on all fours

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:25.139
in your garden and tried to eat a tulip.

00:07:25.139 --> 00:07:30.080
So all these useless memories we create as
an infant are the first to be discarded when

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.289
new memories are created and new skills are
learnt as we grow up.

00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:40.180
So to summarise, we can’t remember being
a baby because our brains have emptied the

00:07:40.180 --> 00:08:08.979
trash, because our hard drive was getting
rather full.

